+### Markup functions and `--no-preamble`
+
+By default the output starts with its own definitions of the markup functions, so it compiles on its own. With `--no-preamble` these definitions are left out, and you must make the names below available yourself, for example with `#import "/diff-markup.typ": *` at the top of each output file.
+
+| Name | Signature | Used for |
+|---|---|---|
+| `diff-added` | `diff-added(body)` | Added text, headings, list items, display equations and raw blocks |
+| `diff-deleted` | `diff-deleted(body)` | Deleted text and blocks |
+| `diff-added-mark` | `diff-added-mark(note: "added")` | Margin mark before an added code block such as `#figure(...)` or `#let` |
+| `diff-deleted-mark` | `diff-deleted-mark(note: "deleted")` | Margin mark where a code block was deleted |
+| `diff-changed-mark` | `diff-changed-mark(note: "changed")` | Margin mark before a changed code block |
+
+Each mark function is called with no arguments, except for `#let` bindings, which pass `note: "definition added"`, `"definition deleted"` or `"definition changed"`. A mark function should use `place()` so that it takes no space in the layout. It must not wrap the next block: the block follows the mark call as a separate element so that its label and number still work.
+
+### How changes to numbered elements are shown
+
+- A label keeps its element. A label on the line after a heading, display equation, raw block or function call stays attached to it in the output. For an added equation the label is inside `#diff-added[...]`, directly after the equation, so `@label` refers to the equation.
+- Deleted content does not take a number and cannot be referenced. A deleted display equation is set with `math.equation(numbering: none)`, a deleted heading is emitted as an unnumbered `#heading(...)`, and the labels of deleted content are dropped. The numbers in the diff therefore match the new document.
+- A changed display equation is shown as the old equation (deleted, unnumbered) followed by the new one (added, numbered).
+- Code blocks such as `#figure(...)`, `#table(...)` and `#let` are not wrapped, because that would change what they do. A changed or added call is emitted as-is after a margin mark. A deleted call is not run: it appears only as `//` comments after a deleted mark, so it takes no figure number and defines nothing. The old version of a changed call is kept as `//` comments too.
+
 ## Features
 
 - **Block-level diffing** — Detects structural changes in headings, paragraphs, list items, enum items, and term list items
